            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The Northern Kingdom of Israel, often symbolizing a rebellious, idolatrous, or backsliding people whom God still deeply loves and desires to restore.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Hosea 4:17\",\"Hosea 11:8\",\"Jeremiah 31:18-20\",\"Isaiah 7:8-9\",\"Isaiah 28:1\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Fruitfulness or double blessing, derived from the literal etymology of the name and Jacob's prophetic blessing.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 41:52\",\"Genesis 48:14-20\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The literal Tribe of Ephraim, representing territorial or tribal inheritance within the historical narrative of Israel rather than a symbolic entity.\",\"basis\":[\"Joshua 16:1-10\",\"Judges 8:1-3\",\"Judges 12:1-6\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The Gentile church or a multitude of nations grafted into God's covenant, interpreting Jacob's blessing of Ephraim as a prophecy of Gentile inclusion.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 48:19\",\"Romans 9:24-26\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In biblical prophetic literature, Ephraim serves as a settled synecdoche for the entire Northern Kingdom of Israel. In pastoral contexts, this prophetic usage often makes Ephraim an archetype for the backsliding believer who faces divine discipline yet remains a recipient of God's enduring paternal love.\"}",
